# Environment variables for local development and deployment
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in your own credentials.

MONGODB_URI=mongodb+srv://user:password@cluster.mongodb.net/apex-voyager
ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=user@example.com
SMTP_PASS=your-smtp-password
NEXT_PUBLIC_WHATSAPP_NUMBER=917307521100
# Optional: overrides the auto-detected host when the homepage falls back to
# /api/tours/featured (e.g. behind a proxy that mangles the Host header).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000
# Server-only — never prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC_, it's billed per request and must
# not reach the browser. Powers /api/destinations (Places API "New" Text Search + Photo Media).
# `next dev` never calls Google with this key at all — lib/googlePlaces.ts and
# app/api/places/photo/route.ts short-circuit to lib/mockPlacesData.ts whenever
# NODE_ENV is 'development' (see lib/env.ts), so it's safe to leave a real key set
# locally. Only `next build`/`next start` and real deployments spend credits.
GOOGLE_PLACES_API_KEY=your-google-places-api-key
# Booking.com Demand API — no credentials exist for this project yet (no token, no
# affiliate ID, no approved API access). Leave unset/false: services/providers/booking/
# only ever returns { enabled: false, accommodations: [] } while this is off, and the
# Region Hub's stays section always falls back to curated MongoDB stays + an enquiry
# CTA. Set to 'true' only once BOOKING_API_TOKEN/BOOKING_AFFILIATE_ID below are real
# and the live Demand API call has actually been wired in.
# BOOKING_API_ENABLED=false
# BOOKING_API_TOKEN=
# BOOKING_AFFILIATE_ID=
# HBX (Hotelbeds) — server-only, never prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC_. Never commit a real
# key or secret here or anywhere else; this file holds placeholders only.
#
# Whatever HOTELBEDS_API_KEY/HOTELBEDS_SECRET point at (evaluation/test OR production)
# is only ever *usable* for real customer-facing pricing once BOTH HBX_PRODUCTION_CONFIRMED
# is 'true' AND that base URL's origin is in the explicit, code-reviewed allow-list at
# config/hbxEnvironment.config.ts (see hbx.client.ts's getHbxEnvironment() and its
# fail-closed design) — evaluation/test credentials and rates must never be exposed to a
# site visitor, and the public pricing resolver (services/pricing/stayPricing.service.ts)
# enforces this regardless of what these env vars say.
#
# Leave HBX_PRODUCTION_CONFIRMED at 'false' until BOTH the official HBX production
# endpoint has been confirmed directly with HBX/Hotelbeds (never guessed — see
# config/hbxEnvironment.config.ts's own doc comment) AND real production account access
# has been verified. Flipping this alone does nothing without also adding that confirmed
# origin to the allow-list — the two are independent by design.
HOTELBEDS_API_BASE_URL=
HOTELBEDS_API_KEY=
HOTELBEDS_SECRET=
HBX_PRODUCTION_CONFIRMED=false
# Server-only — never prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC_. Signs/verifies the short-lived Journey
# quote tokens issued by services/booking/journeyQuote.service.ts (Phase 9B — see the
# Phase 9 audit for the surrounding architecture). A real value must never be committed
# here or anywhere else; this file holds a placeholder only.
#
# Must be a strong, random, server-only secret of at least 32 characters — anything
# shorter, empty, or unset makes the quote service fail closed (createJourneyQuote/
# verifyJourneyQuoteToken both return CONFIGURATION_ERROR rather than sign or accept
# anything). Rotating this value immediately invalidates every outstanding quote token
# still in flight — that's expected, not a bug, since a quote's only integrity guarantee
# comes from this secret.
#
# This needs to be configured separately in each deployment environment (local, preview,
# production) before the quote service becomes reachable there — leaving it unset is
# safe (the service simply refuses to operate) and is the correct default until a real
# booking/payment flow that uses it actually exists.
BOOKING_QUOTE_SECRET=